<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  TAKE EXTENTS, AUDIBLE — THE RESOLUTION RULE (plan 53 §4.1 / A0a, QBX-177).

  take_extents_model gates the model; this gates the one thing the model
  cannot say, which is what comes out of the speaker. Both halves below ARE
  the resolution rule: a column with ANY partial take resolves through
  `twCompColumn` even when its map is EMPTY, because that is the only object
  that knows how to shift a take's position by its tStart and contribute
  NOTHING outside its extent.

  THE SHAPE. One 4 s column over test_sawtooth.wav:
    take 0 — FULL extent [0, 192000), source from 0
    take 1 — PARTIAL [96000, 192000), source from 0 as well

  Take 1 is the interesting one: it sits in the column's SECOND half and reads
  the source from its START. So the same source second arrives at two
  different places depending on which take carries it, and the two takes
  disagree in every window where both have material. Per-source-second RMS of
  the fixture: 0.0667 / 0.1764 / 0.2906 / 0.4055.

  HALF ONE — a MAP over a partial take. Active take 0, map `96000 -> 1`:
    column [0, 2 s)  -> take 0 at 0..2 s  -> 0.0667, 0.1764
    column [2 s, 4 s) -> take 1 AT ITS OWN POSITION 0..2 s -> 0.0667, 0.1764
  so the timeline reads 0.0667 / 0.1764 / 0.0667 / 0.1764 — the first two
  seconds REPEATED, which no build that ignores the extent can produce.

  ON `main` the second half reads 0.2906 / 0.4055 instead: `at`/`length` are
  unknown attributes there, so take 1 is built FULL-extent and the map hands
  it the column position unshifted — the same source second the column is at.
  Measured: window 3 reads 0.29 against an expectation of 0.067.

  HALF TWO — an EMPTY map over a partial ACTIVE take. `select-take take="1"`
  clears the map and activates take 1, and an empty map means "the active take
  everywhere":
    column [0, 2 s)  -> take 1 has NO MATERIAL -> SILENCE
    column [2 s, 4 s) -> take 1 at 0..2 s -> 0.0667, 0.1764

  THE SILENCE IS THE POINT, and it is plan 53 §3.1 step 5 stated as audio:
  there is NO FALL-BACK to another take where the selected one has no
  material, even though take 0 covers that span perfectly well. A build that
  fell back would play 0.0667 / 0.1764 there and look entirely reasonable
  doing it. (Review 2 M2 caught an earlier draft of this very case encoding
  the fall-back as the expectation.)

  ON `main` half two reads take 1 full-extent everywhere: 0.0667 / 0.1764 /
  0.2906 / 0.4055, so the first window fails against silence.

  Both renders are in ONE process, the second over pages and a capture the
  first has already warmed — a column that resolved correctly only from cold
  would pass a case that rendered once.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="take_extents_audible">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- take 1: the column's second half, reading the source from 0.
         activate="0" keeps take 0 active for half one. -->
    <add-take clip="0,0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" startOffset="0"
              index="-1" activate="0" at="96000" length="96000"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="0"
                        extents="0-192000;96000-192000" duration="192000"/>

    <!-- HALF ONE: the map sends the second half to the partial take. -->
    <set-comp-segment clip="0,0" at="96000" take="1"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" comp="96000:1" activeTake="0"/>

    <render filename="tex_mapped.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tex_mapped.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.085"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tex_mapped.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>
    <!-- The two that only an extent-shifted read can produce: the source's
         FIRST two seconds, arriving in the column's SECOND half. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tex_mapped.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.085"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tex_mapped.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>

    <!-- HALF TWO: the map is cleared and the PARTIAL take is the active one.
         select-take is still A0a's — "this take everywhere, map cleared" —
         and that is precisely what makes the uncovered span silent. -->
    <select-take clip="0,0" take="1"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" comp="" activeTake="1"
                        takeAtPos="0" expectTake="-1"/>

    <render filename="tex_partial_active.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <!-- SILENCE over the whole uncovered half, in one assertion rather than
         two: there is nothing to tell apart inside it. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tex_partial_active.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="96000"
                         minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.005"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tex_partial_active.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.085"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tex_partial_active.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>

    <!-- The column did NOT move or shrink: it still owns its whole 4 s even
         though its active take covers half of it. That is what duration_
         exists for — before it, getDuration() answered the active take and
         the clip's right edge would have jumped to 2 s on that click. -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" duration="192000"
                        extents="0-192000;96000-192000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
